      "statement": "From the stated genuine compact weak data and original gradient interface, Derive actual weighted MemLp2 of v,G,A=inner(gradientW,G)-F. Return actual vW,AW,GW L2 classes with all three mu-AE links, (vW,GW) in the SAME D.closure graph, and m*norm(GW)^2<=norm(AW)^2. The actual ordinary smooth sequence, common compact weighted transfer and generator-gradient limits are produced internally. This is compact scalar coercivity, not a rough Hessian, full Bochner/adjoint/operator-core identity, global cutoff result, epsilon-zero/Poincare/BL or complete main theorem.",
      "assumptions": [
        "Finite-dimensional real Hilbert/Borel E, canonical full-space volume, including dimension zero. W is C2, exp(-W) genuinely integrable with positive Z. The actual given measure mu equals volume.tilted(-W), hence is the normalized Gibbs law. The Hessian quadratic form is bounded below by m times squared norm for every point/vector; m is any real number, not necessarily positive.",
        "The original partial real-linear gradient D on actual weighted L2 is closable. Its graph consists exactly of AE classes of C-infinity compact functions and their genuine classical gradients. This is an original structural interface, not a supplied final closure-domain, coercivity, adjoint or core certificate.",
        "Pointwise real v,F and vector G are global ordinary-volume MemLp2 with topological supports in the same compact K. Every real C1 compact psi/every direction a has both legal L1 products and integral psi*inner(G,a)=-integral v*D_a psi. Every real C2 compact psi has both legal L1 products and integral v*Delta psi=integral F*psi. No approximation, weighted class/convergence, domain membership or scalar inequality is an input."
      ],
      "formulae": [
        {
          "label": "Actual compact weak Poisson: original weighted gradient domain and scalar coercivity",
          "tex": "\\mu=Z^{-1}e^{-W}dx,\\quad A=\\langle\\nabla W,G\\rangle-F:\\quad(v_W,G_W)\\in\\operatorname{graph}\\overline D,\\quad m\\|G_W\\|_{L^2(\\mu)}^2\\le\\|A_W\\|_{L^2(\\mu)}^2."
